  </tr>
  <tr>
    <td align="right" valign="top" nowrap><b>Asked by:</b></td>
    <td align="left" colspan="2" valign="top">Steve Hodges</td>
  </tr>
  <tr>
    <td align="right" valign="top" nowrap><b>Subject:</b></td>
    <td align="left" colspan="2" valign="top">Thank you for the site</td>
  </tr>
  <tr>
    <td align=right class=medium valign="top" nowrap><b>Question:</b></td>
    <td align=left class=medium valign="top" width="400" colspan="2">I have looked on the internet for years to see if others like me have made any headway in the rotary motion propulsion field.  Your site with the patents was very up lifting to finally find, though none of the ideas probably could really work that I saw.  I think that there are many of us who realize that it is a reasonable expectation that pumping electrical and kinetic energy into a rotating device, ought to be able to be manipulated in such a way as to produce thrust.  The proto types that I saw remind me of the various ideas I have and tried over the years.  As with everyone I suspect it's a great past time with an aerie of adventure.  One of the caveats that I have added different than any of the other proposed systems I have seen, are a multiple of 2 balanced rotating arms in opposite directions to equalize & balance the overall system.  Also electronic control of the timing & manipulation of the gyroscope or any other mass or device on the end of the spinning arms, that was really fun to work the bugs out of.  Some day some smarter guy than me will stumble on the solution to what we all sense is hidden in the mystery of the spinning wheel.  It will be by accident like the guy who discovered gun cotton.  Thanks again for the site and information you have made public.  By chance have you seen this - I think its a hoax.  http://www.earthfiles.com/news.php?ID=1252&category=Environment</td>
  </tr>
